{"_data": [{"type": "metadata", "contents": [{"content": "A Comedy of Errors", "type": "lesson_name"}, {"content": "Go for It!", "type": "unit_name"}, {"content": 9, "type": "position"}, {"content": "stub", "type": "origin"}, {"content": 10, "type": "lesson"}, {"content": "B2.2", "type": "level"}]}, {"type": "achievment_test", "contents": [{"content": "false", "type": "test"}]}, {"type": "objectives", "contents": [{"content": "Understand detailed descriptions of real or imaginary events and experiences in present, past or future and in spoken or written texts.", "type": "primary"}, {"content": "Understands the main ideas and specific information of complex text on both concrete and abstract topics in written texts in social and work contexts.", "type": "secondary_1"}, {"content": "Understands the main ideas and specific information of complex text on both concrete and abstract topics in written texts in social and work contexts.", "type": "secondary_2"}, {"content": "Understands the main ideas and specific information of complex text on both concrete and abstract topics in written texts in social and work contexts.", "type": "secondary_3"}, {"content": "Understands the main ideas and specific information of complex text on both concrete and abstract topics in written texts in social and work contexts.", "type": "secondary_4"}, {"content": "Understands the main ideas and specific information of complex text on both concrete and abstract topics in written texts in social and work contexts.", "type": "secondary_5"}, {"content": "Understands the main ideas and specific information of complex text on both concrete and abstract topics in written texts in social and work contexts.", "type": "secondary_6"}, {"content": "Understands the main ideas and specific information of complex text on both concrete and abstract topics in written texts in social and work contexts.", "type": "secondary_7"}, {"content": "Understands the main ideas and specific information of complex text on both concrete and abstract topics in written texts in social and work contexts.", "type": "secondary_8"}]}, {"content": "engenn10le10ob09re1img01.jpg", "type": "background_image"}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": "Future Perfect and Future Perfect Continuous Tenses", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "You use the Future Perfect in combination with expressions of time in order to describe an action or an event that will take place before a specific time.</br></br> You use the Future Perfect Continuous in combination with expressions of time in order to describe an event in the future that has not reached an end before another one starts.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "The Future Perfect has two forms:</br> <span class=\"italic\">will have</span> + <span class=\"italic\">past participle</span>, or  </br> <span class=\"italic\">am/is/are + going to have + past participle</span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Unlike Simple Future forms, both forms are interchangeable.</br>  </br>Examples:</br></br>  <span class=\"italic\">By tomorrow, <span class=\"strong\">we will have forgotten</span> about it. </br>By tomorrow, <span class=\"strong\">we're going to have forgotten</span> about it.</span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Here is how you form the Future Perfect Continuous:</br> <span class=\"italic\"> subject + will have been + present participle</span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Example: </br></br><span class=\"italic\"> <span class=\"strong\">I will have been smiling</span> for 48 hours straight by the time you see me on Sunday.</span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Just think, after tonight,<span class=\"strong\"> you will have fallen</span> in love.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "By midnight tonight, this whole thing <span class=\"strong\">will have blown over</span>.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "By tomorrow, <span class=\"strong\">we will have forgotten </span>all about it.", "type": "example"}, {"content": " By Sunday, we <span class=\"strong\">will have been laughing </span>about it like it was a distant memory.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "I<span class=\"strong\"> will have been talking and carrying </span>on for hours on end. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "By tomorrow, we <span class=\"strong\">will have completely fallen head over heels </span>for each other.", "type": "example"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": " The Present Real and Unreal Conditionals", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "You can use the Present Real Conditional to describe cause and effect relationships between real events or situations.</br></br>You can use the Present Unreal Conditional to describe cause and effect relationships between hypothetical events or situations.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "The Present Real Conditional is also called the Zero Conditional. </br>You use the structure:</br><span class=\"italic\"><span class=\"strong\">  If/when</span> ... Simple Present, ... Simple Present</br></span> or <span class=\"italic\">Simple Present ...<span class=\"strong\"> if/when </span>... Simple Present</span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "In the Present Real Conditional you can introduce a condition using either \u201cif\u201d or \u201cwhen\u201d.</br>When you use \u201cif\u201d you are trying to say that the result of the condition may or may not come true.</br>When you use \u201cwhen\u201d you are trying to say that the result of the condition always comes true.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "You use the <span class=\"strong\">Present Simple</span> tense to write the zero conditional. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "The Present Unreal Conditional is also called the Second Conditional. </br>You use the structure:  </br><span class=\"italic\"><span class=\"strong\">If</span>... Simple Past, ... would + verb </span>or</br> <span class=\"italic\">Would + verb ...<span class=\"strong\"> if </span>... Simple Past</span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Example:  <span class=\"italic\">If Sally were here, she would be able to give you better advice. </span></br>It is impossible for Sally to be here. The condition is not real.  </br></br><span class=\"italic\">If I were you, I wouldn't get my hopes up. </br></br></span> You use <span class=\"italic\">If I were you ...</span> to give a strong piece of advice.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "When you have a big disappointment, you often get melancholy.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "If Sally were here, she would be able to give you better advice.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "If Sally were here, she would have blogged about this as if it were a boxing match.", "type": "example"}, {"content": " If you need me, I'll be here.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "If I go on a date, I usually lower my expectations.", "type": "example"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "disenchanted", "type": "name"}, {"content": "disappointed", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective) ", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: I am disenchanted with the whole dating process.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn10le10ob09aud01", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word_describer", "contents": [{"content": "These are words you use when you are disappointed.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "melancholy", "type": "name"}, {"content": "feeling sad, and perhaps depressed", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective) ", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: That song has so much melancholy and sadness.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn10le10ob09aud02", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "to calibrate", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to determine, check, or rectify", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb) ", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: I have calibrated the site on my rifle. Now it is accurate.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn10le10ob09aud03", 